  "summary": "This style also can be worn without associating with the Jirai Kei subculture, and people who do not want to describe their style as that use the label \"dark girly\" or \"sweet girly\" instead, including by those in Japan.",
  "description": "This style also can be worn without associating with the Jirai Kei subculture, and people who do not want to describe their style as that use the label \"dark girly\" or \"sweet girly\" instead, including by those in Japan. Alternatively, \"fashion Jirai\" or \"wearing Jirai Kei\" is also popular. The labels \"dark girly\" and \"sweet girly\" exist in one page, as these two styles overlap, with only the accessories and garment color being the determiner of \"sweet\" versus \"dark,\" and these two styles more often than not coexist in one wardrobe.\n\nAs a note, the style is often called \"Ryousangata\" (meaning mass-produced) in the West when the outfits are in white and pink colorways and lack edgier hardware details and instead use gem charms (sweet girly). The term actually changed throughout Japanese fashion history, and generally refers to the trends Japanese women adopt and look similar to each other, whether in Uniqlo basics or in Gyaru. However, still using this term in the 2020s will be understandable, including to those in Japan, as this style is the current version of Ryousangata.",
  "culturalContext": "Key values (per the Aesthetics Wiki): Femininity contrasted with edginess",
